# Sync unit files from the repo, so a .service change deploys like code.
#
# WHY THIS IS A SEPARATE UNIT FROM marketslap-update. That one runs as
# `marketslap` and must stay that way — it pulls code from a public
# repo and has no business writing outside /opt/marketslap. This one
# writes to /etc/systemd/system and calls daemon-reload, so it runs as
# root, and keeping them apart is what stops the pull itself from being
# a root operation.
#
# IT RUNS AFTER THE PULL, NOT ALONGSIDE IT. Syncing unit files from a
# half-updated checkout would install a unit for code that has not
# arrived, so the ordering is explicit rather than left to two timers
# happening to fire in a useful order.
#
# THE TRADE THIS MAKES, stated plainly: anything that reaches
# deploy/marketslap-*.service on main becomes a root-run unit on this
# box within ten minutes. That is an escalation from the `marketslap`
# user to root for anyone who can push to the repo.
#
# It is a SMALL escalation, because that account already holds the
# service-role key and already runs whatever main says. Root buys
# persistence, not access to the data. Weighed against three unit
# defects in two days that each needed a human at a terminal, and a
# recorder whose crash-loop ceiling was silently absent on every box,
# the exposure is worth closing the gap. Revisit if the repo ever gains
# contributors who are not trusted with the database.

[Unit]
Description=MarketSlap systemd unit sync
After=marketslap-update.service
Requires=marketslap-update.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/usr/bin/env DIR=/opt/marketslap /bin/bash /opt/marketslap/deploy/sync-units.sh
